{"name":"Lavender Soap","hex":"#f1bfe2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lavender","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a group of European plants, genus, Lavandula, of the mint family.","A pale bluish purple or violet colour, like that of the lavender flower."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour.","Pertaining to LGBT people and rights."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lavender"},{"term":"soap","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A metallic salt derived from a fatty acid, commonly used in cleaning products.","Some other substance, often a detergent or another surfactant, able to mix with both oil and water, used for cleaning."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply soap to in washing.","To cover, lather, or in any other manner treat with soap, often as a prank."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soap"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lavandula","blocks":[{"text":"Lavandula is a genus of 47 known species of perennial flowering plants in the sage family, Lamiaceae. It is native to regions of the Iberian Peninsula and Mediterranean coastline, the Middle East, extending to the Indian subcontinent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Single_lavender_flower02.jpg/330px-Single_lavender_flower02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavandula","wikidata":"Q171892"},{"title":"Soap","blocks":[{"text":"Soap is a salt of a fatty acid used for cleaning and lubricating products as well as other applications. In a domestic setting, soaps, specifically \"toilet soaps\", are surfactants typically used for washing, bathing, and other types of housekeeping. In industrial settings, soaps are used as thickeners, components of some lubricants, emulsifiers, and catalysts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Handmade_soap_cropped_and_simplified.jpg/